  <h1>Lesson 23: Data Types & Operators</h1>
  <p>What Type of Data? Number, Text, True / False + Maths in JS | LearnHTMLCSSJS.com</p>
</header>

<div class="container">
  <div class="tabs">
    <button id="lessonTabBtn" class="tab-btn active" onclick="showLesson()">📖 Lesson</button>
    <button id="testTabBtn" class="tab-btn" onclick="showTest()">📝 Test - 100 Marks</button>
  </div>
  <div id="lockMsg" class="lock-msg">🔒 Test is running! All tabs are locked until you submit the test or time ends.</div>

  <div id="lessonContent">
    <h2>1. What is a Data Type?</h2>
    <p><b>Data Type = The Type of Data.</b> Every value in JavaScript has a type. It tells JavaScript what kind of data it is.</p>
    <p>Example: <b>"Ali"</b> is Text, <b>20</b> is a Number, <b>true</b> is Yes / No. JavaScript needs to know the type to work correctly with the value.</p>
    <h2>2. LIVE DEMO - Mini Calculator</h2>
    <p>Type two numbers and press a button. This is how operators work:</p>
    <div class="demo-card">
      <h3 id="calcResult" style="margin:0">Result: -</h3>
      <p style="color:#64748b;margin:8px 0">Try: 10 and 3</p>
      <input class="demo-input" id="num1" type="number" placeholder="First Number - e.g. 10" value="10">
      <input class="demo-input" id="num2" type="number" placeholder="Second Number - e.g. 3" value="3">
      <button class="btn btn-js" onclick="doCalc('+')">+ Add</button>
      <button class="btn btn-next" onclick="doCalc('-')">- Subtract</button>
      <button class="btn" onclick="doCalc('*')">× Multiply</button>
      <button class="btn btn-js" onclick="doCalc('/')">÷ Divide</button>
      <button class="btn btn-next" onclick="doCalc('%')">% Remainder</button>
      <p id="calcType" style="margin-top:10px;font-size:13px;color:#64748b">Type check will show here</p>
    </div>
    <h2>3. 5 Main Data Types</h2>
    <table>
      <tr><th>Data Type</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th><th>Quotes?</th></tr>
      <tr><td><b>String</b></td><td>Text / Words</td><td>"Ali", "Hello", "Pakistan"</td><td>✅ Yes - " "</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Number</b></td><td>Any Number</td><td>20, 99.5, -10, 0</td><td>❌ No</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Boolean</b></td><td>Yes / No - True / False</td><td>true, false</td><td>❌ No</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Undefined</b></td><td>Box is empty - No value given yet</td><td>let x; // undefined</td><td>❌ No</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Null</b></td><td>Empty on purpose</td><td>let y = null;</td><td>❌ No</td></tr>
    </table>
    <div class="code-box"><code>
<span class="text">let name = "Ali"; // String - Text in quotes</span><br>
<span class="text">let age = 20; // Number - No quotes</span><br>
<span class="text">let price = 99.5; // Number - Decimal is also a Number</span><br>
<span class="text">let isPassed = true; // Boolean - true / false</span><br>
<span class="text">let isFail = false; // Boolean</span><br>
<span class="text">let x; // Undefined - No value yet</span><br>
<span class="text">let y = null; // Null - Empty on purpose</span>
    </code></div>
    <h2>4. typeof - Check the Type</h2>
    <p><b>typeof</b> tells us what type a variable is.</p>
    <div class="code-box"><code>
<span class="text">typeof "Ali"; // "string"</span><br>
<span class="text">typeof 20; // "number"</span><br>
<span class="text">typeof true; // "boolean"</span><br>
<span class="text">typeof x; // "undefined"</span>
    </code></div>
    <h2>5. Operators - Maths in JavaScript</h2>
    <p><b>Operator = A Symbol That Does Some Work.</b> Just like + - x / in a calculator.</p>
    <table>
      <tr><th>Operator</th><th>Name</th><th>Example</th><th>Result</th></tr>
      <tr><td><b>+</b></td><td>Add / Join</td><td>10 + 3</td><td>13</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>-</b></td><td>Subtract</td><td>10 - 3</td><td>7</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>*</b></td><td>Multiply</td><td>10 * 3</td><td>30</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>/</b></td><td>Divide</td><td>10 / 3</td><td>3.33</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>%</b></td><td>Remainder (Left Over)</td><td>10 % 3</td><td>1</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>**</b></td><td>Power</td><td>2 ** 3</td><td>8</td></tr>
    </table>
    <div class="note"><b>% (Remainder) Explained:</b> 10 / 3 = 3, remainder = 1. So 10 % 3 = 1. This is useful to check Even / Odd numbers. If number % 2 == 0, the number is Even.</div>
    <h2>6. + Has Two Jobs - Important Trick!</h2>
    <p><b>+</b> adds Numbers, but it joins Strings.</p>
    <div class="code-box"><code>
<span class="text">10 + 20; // 30 - Number + Number = Add</span><br>
<span class="text">"Ali" + " Khan"; // "Ali Khan" - String + String = Join</span><br>
<span class="text">"Age: " + 20; // "Age: 20" - String + Number = Join</span><br>
<span class="text">"10" + 20; // "1020" - "10" is a String, so it Joins, it does NOT Add!</span><br>
<span class="text">10 + 20 + " Ali"; // "30 Ali" - First Add, then Join</span>
    </code></div>
    <div class="note"><b>Biggest Beginner Mistake:</b> Value from an input is always a <b>String</b>. <code>"10" + "20" = "1020"</code>, not <code>30</code>. So we must convert it to a Number first - <code>Number("10") + Number("20") = 30</code>. In the calculator demo above, we did the same thing.</div>
    <h2>7. Comparison Operators - Compare Two Values</h2>
    <p>The answer is always <b>true</b> or <b>false</b>.</p>
    <table>
      <tr><th>Operator</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th><th>Result</th></tr>
      <tr><td><b>==</b></td><td>Equal? (Value only)</td><td>10 == "10"</td><td>true</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>===</b></td><td>Equal? (Value + Type)</td><td>10 === "10"</td><td>false</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>!=</b></td><td>Not Equal?</td><td>10 != 20</td><td>true</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>&gt;</b></td><td>Greater Than</td><td>10 &gt; 5</td><td>true</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>&lt;</b></td><td>Less Than</td><td>10 &lt; 5</td><td>false</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>&gt;=</b></td><td>Greater or Equal</td><td>80 &gt;= 80</td><td>true (Pass!)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>&lt;=</b></td><td>Less or Equal</td><td>50 &lt;= 80</td><td>true</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p><b>Golden Rule:</b> Always use <b>===</b>, not <b>==</b>. <code>===</code> also checks the type, so it is safer.</p>
    <h2>8. Complete Example - Pass / Fail Checker</h2>
    <div class="code-box"><code>
<span class="tag">&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;</span><br>
<span class="tag">&lt;html&gt;</span><br>
<span class="tag">&lt;body&gt;</span><br><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="tag">&lt;h1</span> <span class="attr">id</span>=<span class="val">"result"</span><span class="tag">&gt;</span><span class="text">Enter Your Marks</span><span class="tag">&lt;/h1&gt;</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="tag">&lt;input</span> <span class="attr">id</span>=<span class="val">"marks"</span> <span class="attr">type</span>=<span class="val">"number"</span> <span class="attr">placeholder</span>=<span class="val">"Your Marks - e.g. 85"</span><span class="tag">&gt;</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="tag">&lt;button</span> <span class="attr">onclick</span>=<span class="val">"check()"</span><span class="tag">&gt;</span><span class="text">Check Result</span><span class="tag">&lt;/button&gt;</span><br><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="tag">&lt;script&gt;</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">function check() {</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">let marks = Number(document.getElementById("marks").value);</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">let total = marks + 10; // Add 10 bonus</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">let isPass = marks >= 80; // true / false</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">document.getElementById("result").innerHTML = "Total: " + total + " | Pass: " + isPass + " | Type: " + typeof marks;</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">}</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="tag">&lt;/script&gt;</span><br>
<span class="tag">&lt;/body&gt;</span><br>
<span class="tag">&lt;/html&gt;</span>
    </code></div>
    <h2>9. Homework</h2>
    <p>In Playground, build this:</p>
    <p>1. Two inputs - Price and Quantity. On button click, show <b>Total = Price * Quantity</b><br>2. Use <b>typeof</b> to check: <code>typeof "Ali"</code>, <code>typeof 20</code>, <code>typeof true</code> - show them in the console<br>3. One input - Age. Check if <b>Age &gt;= 18</b> or not - show true / false</p>
    <div class="box"><b>Remember:</b> String = "Text" in quotes | Number = 20, no quotes | Boolean = true / false | typeof = Check the type | + = Add (Number) / Join (String) | % = Remainder | === = Strict Equal (Value + Type) | Input value = Always a String, use Number() to convert it</div>
    <p><b>Next Step:</b> <a href="/lesson24">Lesson 24 - If-Else</a> - Learn decision making. If you pass, do this, otherwise do that.</p>
    <a id="playgroundLink" class="btn" href="/playground">▶ Try it Yourself - Playground</a><br><br>
